Lag-2 is the primary lag (as approved); lags 1 and 3 and the contemporaneous term are robustness and
all keep the same negative sign in levels; the differences specification keeps a positive sign.
Interpretation: **-0.20 ln points (~-18% relative to trend) per +1pp of GNI devoted to education in
trend-adjusted levels, versus +0.02 to +0.04 ln points of annual growth (~+2 to +4%) per +1pp in
year-to-year changes.** Sign flips with the frequency band used.

## Step C - group comparison
**C1 pooled, group intercept + SSA x edu interaction (N=46 = 41 LMC + 5 SSA) — REFERENCE ONLY, VERY LOW POWER:**
LMC slope -0.4466 (0.2947, p=0.130); SSA intercept -0.893 (p=0.411); **interaction +0.3466 (0.2961,
p=0.242)** -> implied SSA slope -0.10. Not significant; the interaction is identified from 5 SSA income
observations and must not be read as evidence of equality or of difference.
**C2 common window 2011-2015 (only window where both groups have income data, n=5 each):**
SSA education share 3.65 -> 3.15 (mean 3.41) vs LMC 3.23 -> 3.06 (mean 3.16); income growth
**SSA +5.1% vs LMC +13.8%** over the same 4 years; slope b(ln Y ~ edu) SSA -0.10, LMC -0.66;
r(Δln Y, Δ edu) SSA -0.47, LMC -0.89. SSA's 2015 GNI/capita (1634) equals LMC's **2009** level, and the
level gap widened from 171 (2011) to 331 (2015) US$.

## Step C3 - mechanism (education -> enrolment; 41 yrs available for BOTH groups)
Levels: SSA primary GER ~ edu = **-10.34 (3.06, p=0.001)***, SSA secondary **-7.20 (3.04, p=0.018)**;
LMC primary -8.95 (p=0.227), LMC secondary -14.45 (p=0.232). Pooled interactions ns
(primary -1.39, p=0.86; secondary +7.25, p=0.56).
Differences: LMC secondary ΔGER ~ Δedu = **-0.786 (0.290, p=0.007)***, LMC primary -0.781 (p=0.088)*;
SSA secondary -0.264 (p=0.213), SSA primary **+0.249 (p=0.590)**; pooled SSA differential +0.52
(p=0.147) secondary, +1.03 (p=0.112) primary — all ns, but point the same way: no detectable
enrolment payoff to spending changes in SSA, a mildly negative one in LMC.
LMC decade cross-check (edu mean -> decade growth): 1970s 3.51 -> -0.3%; 1980s 3.47 -> 13.5%;
1990s **3.71 (highest) -> 14.4%**; 2000s 3.52 -> 39.2%; 2010s 3.19 -> 16.8%: the decade with the
highest education-spending share is not the decade with the highest income growth.

## Step D - secondary specification with controls (1991-2015 only, SSA/controls unavailable earlier)
LMC, ln Y ~ edu(lag2): -0.981 (p<0.001)*** -> with primary+secondary GER: **-0.047 (p=0.523, ns)**;
full controls (GER, exports, LFPR): **-0.162 (0.064, p=0.011)** with GER +0.019/+0.022 (p<0.001),
exports -0.003 (p<0.001), LFPR +0.075 (p<0.001). Differences on the same restricted window:
Δedu -0.031 (p=0.484) or +0.012 (p=0.717) with exports. So (i) most of the negative level association
is absorbed by enrolment/demographics, and (ii) **the positive differences result of B4/B5 does NOT
survive shortening the sample to 1991-2015** — it is a whole-period (40-difference) feature.

## Limitations
1. SSA income exists for **5 years only (2011-2015)**: any SSA slope (C1, C2) is descriptive, not an
   estimate; no inference is attached to it.
2. Controls (exports, LFPR) force a 1991-2015 window (N=25) and reverse/erase the differences result.
3. **SSA is largely a subset of the LMC aggregate**: this is a "subset vs broader aggregate" contrast,
   which **attenuates any slope difference toward zero**. Non-significance of the SSA interaction is
   therefore a limitation, not evidence that the two groups behave alike.
4. Group aggregates (N=2 panels, 41 waves): no country fixed effects, no identification strategy;
   simultaneity (income funds spending), and the treatment is a **ratio with income in the
   denominator**, which mechanically biases level associations downward. Endogeneity of spending is
   unaddressed.
5. GNI per capita (2015 US$) is used because GDP per capita is absent; education spending is
   adjusted savings (% of GNI), not % of government expenditure.
6. Residual autocorrelation is high in levels specifications (DW 0.2-0.5, AR(1) 0.74-0.89) even with
   HAC; levels coefficients should be read as trend-conditional correlations, not dynamics.

